# TODO - Performance regression contracts

## Objective

Measure useful throughput without degrading fidelity.

## Acceptance

- Measure frame times, FPS, latency, memory, loading and binary size.
- Use representative workloads and comparable quality settings.
- Keep GPU acceleration, CPU work and cache budgets workload-driven.

## Evidence

Partial evidence, 2026-11-24: fresh validation passed in run
1795575501611655948-2483905 (72 tests).

`games::performance::PerformanceCollector` aggregates frame durations, FPS,
input latency, resident-memory peaks, loading duration and binary size
from explicit host observations in constant memory. One bounded timing
summary provides sample counts, totals, maxima and nanosecond means for
both frame and input measurements. Missing input or memory measurements
remain absent rather than being fabricated as zero.

Synthetic tests verify units, measured zero versus absent data, maximum
representable durations, overflow atomicity (including partial frame
updates) and unchanged quality settings. The fixed-step clock computes
eligible ticks directly instead of scanning the backlog before updates.

No representative playable game, actual GPU timings or reproducible
platform benchmark matrix exists yet. Hardware-specific optimization
choices and acceptance measurements remain open.

The collector now records the explicit capped or uncapped presentation
policy in the quality profile. A caller may configure a separate nonzero
frame-time target; deadline misses are counted exactly in constant memory.
A frame at the target is not counted as missed, and rejected observations
cannot change the miss count. Tests demonstrate a 50 FPS mean with one
over-budget frame and confirm that a rendering cap alone does not imply a
benchmark deadline. Fresh validation passed in run
1795577195880786433-2782349 (91 workspace tests).

Controller detachment now uses the held-input map's controller-specific
key range rather than repeatedly scanning unrelated input sources. A test
covers both extreme controller identifiers, shared logical actions and
stable release order. Fresh validation passed in run
1795577450698187130-2832751 (95 workspace tests). No benchmark speedup is
claimed from this algorithmic change alone.

The native platform meter connects monotonic host completion timestamps
to the shared collector and rejects reversed intervals without changing
the previous timestamp. Load intervals and actual artifact byte sizes are
measured separately. Linux procfs resident-memory sampling is explicit
and reports invalid or unavailable data rather than a fabricated value.
Tests include the real test executable and Linux process resident size.
The native tests passed in fresh run 1795577633179381736-2865763.
Host completion age is not a physical display or GPU latency measurement.
Android procfs availability and actual game workloads remain unverified.

The Linux graphics adapter uses one bounded streaming RGBA texture for a
presentation session. The shared viewport determines the drawable fit,
and SDL uses nearest-neighbor sampling without requested vertical sync.
A display-independent software-canvas read-back verifies all pixels in
a scaled, letterboxed frame and a subsequent upload; a rejected short
frame leaves the previous pixels unchanged. An explicitly enabled hidden
native window also presented two frames and reported SDL's accelerated
renderer capability. Fresh validation with GAMES_NATIVE_RENDER_SMOKE=1
passed in run 1795582311633882091-3468901 (164 workspace tests).
The host emitted a nonfatal libdecor plugin initialization warning.
The capability flag does not prove physical GPU execution, measured
frame latency, scene throughput or title parity.

The native graphics adapter now also composes ordered sprite scenes into
a reusable logical render target. Static RGBA atlases are uploaded once;
per-frame work is bounded by an explicit sprite-command limit. The atlas
count and logical RGBA byte budget are independent. It shares the existing
letterbox and nearest-sampling policy with streaming frame presentation.
Exact software-renderer read-back covers draw order, transparency,
horizontal flipping, clipping, frame clearing and rejection without
changing the displayed pixels. Both rendering paths passed the explicitly
enabled native accelerated-window smoke in fresh validation run
1795582523569847945-3501164. No physical GPU timing, actual VRAM usage or
game-scene throughput is claimed.

An opt-in native diagnostic now uses the bounded persistent atlas path
with actual locally imported data. Exact logical RGBA readback matched
the CPU-packed atlas in two consecutive frames for each of two selections
(1,024 background tiles and 1,023 sprite tiles) on SDL OpenGL.
Readback is explicitly diagnostic and synchronises the renderer; it is
not part of the ordinary presentation loop. Physical GPU utilisation,
frame-time percentiles and peak resident/VRAM usage remain unmeasured.

The native-preview `mame render-bench` command now reuses the same verified
atlas and logical-target comparison, warms eight frames, and measures a
bounded count of subsequent presentations without readback. It reports
host completion intervals, a 60 Hz budget, load time, sampled process RSS
and executable size. The renderer name is copied before SDL teardown.

Two release-mode trials of 600 frames per scene on GNU/Linux x86-64,
Xeon E5-2690 v3, Rust 1.100.0 and system SDL 2.32.74 (OpenGL):

- 256x256, 1,024 draws: mean 389,705–389,926 ns, 2,564–2,566
  submissions/s, max 5,570,542 ns, load 199,759,508–238,941,157 ns.
- 512x512, 1,023 draws: mean 380,157–409,538 ns, 2,441–2,630
  submissions/s, max 4,653,871 ns, load 294,700,846–324,528,021 ns.
- All 2,400 measured frames met the 16,666,667 ns budget. Sampled RSS
  ranged from 168,341,504 to 175,837,184 bytes across trials; the diagnostic
  executable was 1,108,952 bytes (not a packaged game).

This is a hidden, uncapped, static imported-asset workload, not a
representative moving game scene. Submission throughput is not physical
display FPS. Actual GPU utilisation, VRAM, input-to-photon latency,
frame percentiles and true peak memory still need measurement.

The collector now optionally allocates two fixed 736-counter histograms
for frame durations and measured input ages. Nearest-rank percentile
ranges use eight subdivisions per doubling, resolving 0–7 ns exactly.
Ordinary collectors allocate neither histogram. Tests compare every
integer percentile with exact nearest-rank samples, distinguish missing
and zero input ages, cover the largest representable duration and preserve
the entire distribution on rejected observations. The native frame meter
transfers collector ownership without copying sample counters.

The release diagnostic now reports p50, p95 and p99 inclusive ranges.
Two further 600-frame trials of the same 1,024-draw 256x256 hidden atlas
scene yielded:

- p50 and p95: 360,448–393,215 ns in both trials.
- p99: 425,984–458,751 ns and 458,752–491,519 ns.
- Mean: 389,190–404,121 ns; all 1,200 frames met the 60 Hz target.

These are host completion intervals; input ages, physical display latency
and GPU/VRAM use were not measured. The static workload is not a game.
The shared tests passed in fresh run 1795584639072641475-3814930.

The native-preview command `mame scene-sequence-bench` requires 2–64
explicit aligned observation groups and bounded repetitions (at most
20,000 measured frames). It checks every original screen pixel before
admission and rejects both static and mismatched corpora. It shares the
existing scene reconstruction and timing collector, retains verified tile
and sprite payloads once, and reconstructs each measured frame into reused
buffers for a persistent native streaming texture.

Two release trials cycled 31 aligned 320x224 Simpsons observations 20
times each after warmup (620 measured frames per trial). Every scene
matched all 71,680 pixels before measurement. Linux SDL OpenGL, uncapped:

- Mean: 3,002,352–3,018,901 ns; 331–333 submissions/s.
- p50: 2,883,584–3,145,727 ns; p95: 3,145,728–3,407,871 ns.
- p99: 3,407,872–3,670,015 ns; maximum: 10,441,837 ns.
- No misses in either trial against the 60 Hz target.
- Load: 412,745,728–544,226,856 ns; sampled peak RSS:
  170,344,448–171,089,920 bytes; executable: 1,278,952 bytes.

Timed work includes CPU reconstruction and native submission, not
repeated archive I/O or reference readback. A separate single-scene
native readback matched all pixels; duplicate and misaligned corpora
were rejected. These observations are not live gameplay, display FPS,
physical input latency, GPU timing or VRAM measurements.

The background compositor now resolves tile-cell attributes, palette and
source row once per visible tile span instead of once per pixel. Its
inline span test crosses the 512-pixel wrap, multiple tiles and clipped
end spans, preserving transparent pixels and accumulated priority bits.
The same 31-scene exact admission and two 620-frame release trials passed:

- Mean: 766,602–772,096 ns (previously 3,002,352–3,018,901 ns).
- p50: 720,896–786,431 ns; p95: 786,432–851,967 ns.
- p99: 1,179,648–1,441,791 ns across both trials.
- Maximum: 5,311,853 ns; 1,295–1,304 submissions/s; no budget misses.
- Load: 345,806,907–482,435,234 ns; sampled peak RSS:
  170,418,176–170,795,008 bytes; executable: 1,279,048 bytes.

The measured mean submission interval decreased by about 74% at the
same logical extent, scene corpus and renderer settings. These timings
include presentation overhead and remain host-side diagnostics.

The title's hidden native smoke now measures 600 fixed-step movement
frames with four active stations, alternating horizontal directions every
30 ticks. The shared percentile collector measures simulation plus native
sprite submission; actual gameplay time remains independent of wall time.
Two release-mode trials with the touch panel, verified external assets,
and dummy audio completed all frames without missing the 60 Hz budget:

- Mean: 64,701 ns and 69,938 ns.
- p95 inclusive: 65,536–73,727 ns in both trials.
- Maximum: 3,175,987 ns and 3,215,266 ns.

This is a controlled movement slice without an active enemy; combat and
audio are exercised separately by the smoke. No physical display FPS,
GPU execution time, input latency or memory claim follows from this test.
A third release run with per-station position checks passed (600 frames,
66,188 ns mean, 3,130,330 ns maximum, zero misses). Fresh validation
passed in run 1795593147156550715-1327130.
